debug>

  (defun ensure-symbol (name package intern recycle shadowed imported inherited exported)
    (check-type name string)
    (check-type package package)
    (check-type intern (member nil t)) ; no cl:boolean on Genera
    (check-type shadowed hash-table)
    (check-type imported hash-table)
    (check-type inherited hash-table)
    (unless (or (gethash name shadowed)
                (gethash name imported)
                (gethash name inherited))
      (multiple-value-bind (existing status)
          (find-symbol name package)
        (multiple-value-bind (recycled previous) (recycle-symbol name recycle exported)
          (cond
            ((and status (eq existing recycled) (eq previous package)))
            (previous
             (rehome-symbol recycled package))
            ((and status (eq package (symbol-package existing))))
            (t
             (when status
               (note-package-fishiness
                :ensure-symbol name
                (reify-package (symbol-package existing) package)
                status intern)
               (unintern existing))
             (when intern
               (intern* name package))))))))

It looks like find-symbol is too picky with its package argument.  It
is a package-designator, which is a string-designator for a package
name or a package itself.

Since string-designators are (or string symbol character)
package-designator is (or pacakge string symbol character)
(find-symbol "foo" #\A) searches for A::|foo|
(find-symbol "BAR" :poo) searches for poo::bar
(find-symbol "Quux" "Muux") searches for |Muux|::|Quux|
and (find-symbol "MOO" *package*) searches for moo in the current package.

1- correct find-symbol (assuming my diagnostic of the cause of the
   above error is correct).

2- for all functions in CL, lookup in clhs the definitions of its
   arguments types, and verify if the function accepts all the specified
   types (special care for designator types!).

RESOLUTION (2026-07-15) — v1.8.49
---------------------------------
1. FIND-SYMBOL (and INTERN, UNINTERN, EXPORT, ...) already accept the full
   package designator — package object, string, symbol, character — on the
   current vm head: packages.c resolves every package argument through
   package_find() / string_designator_name(), and the original repro
   (loading modules/asdf/asdf.lisp) passes since the uiop-bocl-porting work
   (v1.8.40 predates it).  Note (find-symbol "T" #\T) correctly errors:
   #\T designates the package NAME "T", and no such package exists.

2. Designator audit across the CL functions (CLHS argument types):
   probed string / package / function / stream / pathname / character /
   condition / restart designators over ~120 call shapes.  Findings, all
   fixed:
   - STREAM DESIGNATORS: the C stream built-ins used the stream argument
     raw, so an explicit NIL (standard stream of the direction) or T
     (*TERMINAL-IO*) type-errored in PRINT/PRIN1/PRINC/TERPRI/READ-CHAR/
     PEEK-CHAR/READ/READ-PRESERVING-WHITESPACE/READ-DELIMITED-LIST/
     FINISH-OUTPUT/FORCE-OUTPUT/WRITE-CHAR/%STREAM-LINE-START-P — and
     through them in the prelude wrappers (READ-LINE, LISTEN, FRESH-LINE,
     WRITE-LINE, READ-CHAR-NO-HANG...).  Added stream_designator_input/
     output() resolvers in stream.c (NIL -> *STANDARD-INPUT*/-OUTPUT*,
     T -> *TERMINAL-IO*, falling back to the standard stream while
     *TERMINAL-IO* is NIL), used by stream_input/output_cstream() and by
     every stream built-in at argument-pop time; printer.c's private
     resolver now delegates to it.
   - UNREAD-CHAR did not exist.  Implemented in C (one-character ungetc
     pushback per CLHS, unreading #\Newline pushes the device newline
     byte so the reader's NEL mapping round-trips).
   - Everything else conformed: string functions take string designators,
     package functions take package designators, sequence/apply functions
     take 'symbol function designators, ERROR/SIGNAL/WARN/CERROR take
     condition designators, INVOKE-RESTART/FIND-RESTART take restart
     designators, pathname functions take namestrings.
   New CUnit suite tests/test_designators.c (43rd suite) locks all of it.

3. Found while auditing, tracked separately:
   issues/open/gc-repl-handler-case-heap-corruption.issue — the audit
   probe stream crashes the REPL under GC (BOCL_NOGC=1 passes 121/121);
   the audit itself was validated GC-off and the committed test suite
   (which uses bocl_test_eval, not the REPL path) passes GC-on.
